.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_FULL 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_full \- declare and create an ordered map with custom allocation and comparison
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_full(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs,
                     custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro, and then creates a map that takes its memory from mmgmt_procs and
orders its keys with custom_comparison_proc.

This is the widest of the construct macros. cbmap_construct(3) is this
macro with the default allocator and the default order,
cbmap_construct_mp(3) gives only an allocator, and cbmap_construct_cc(3)
gives only a comparison procedure. Each of the two arguments can be NULL,
which selects the matching default, so this one macro covers every
combination.

The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails.
When the caller must handle a failure and the failure must not be fatal,
use cbmap_init_full(3) on a handle that you declared before.
cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3) is the same macro with a cleanup attribute,
which destroys the map at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                   Key type. It tells every later macro how to read
                        a key, and tells the container which width to
                        expect and which alignment to give a stored key
val_t                   Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read
                        it; the library never gives it to the container
mmgmt_procs             Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the
                        default allocator
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure gets pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value when
the first key orders before the second key, a positive value when the
first key orders after the second key, and zero when the map must treat
the two keys as the same key. A zero makes a call an update instead of an
insert, so the procedure defines both key equality and key order.

The procedure must give a strict weak order over key_t, and that order must
not change while the map holds elements: the same pair of keys must always
compare in the same way, a comparison in the opposite direction must give
the opposite sign, and the order must be transitive. An inconsistent
procedure breaks the placement invariant of the tree, after which the map
cannot find a key that it holds.

Because the procedure gets two bare pointers and no lengths, it can only
read a width that key_t fixes. cbmap_create_full(3) describes the default
orders that a procedure replaces, and the order that a struct key type
gets without a procedure.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
When the caller gives a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, none of its four members
may be NULL. The map copies the procedures into itself, but the
procedures and the comparison procedure must stay callable for the whole
life of the map
Every tree node that the map allocates comes from these procedures, and
everything that the map frees goes back to them
The walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order that the
map got. cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract
Destroy the map with cbmap_destroy(3), or use cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3)
if you want the exit of the scope to destroy it instead
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a function
that receives the handle needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};
int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b);

cbmap_construct_full(releases, version, char *, &procs, version_cmp);
cbmap_destroy(releases);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_construct (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_mp (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_cc (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_full_scoped (3),
.BR cbmap_init_full (3),
.BR cbmap_declare (3),
.BR cbmap_redeclare (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3)
